ione]","Cell_line":"HaCaT","Time(h)":24,"IC50_Dark(M*10^-6)":">50","IC50_Dark_value":50,"Method":"MTT","DOI":"10.1039/d5md00360a","Year":2025,"Counterion":"CC(=O)C=C(C)[O-].CC(=O)C=C(C)[O-]","Type":"IC50"}],"logp":[{"abbreviation_in_the_article":"[Co(III)-dione]","value":0.89,"value_relation":"=","type_authors":"logP","method":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1039/d5md00360a","year":2025,"counterion":"CC(=O)C=C(C)[O-].CC(=O)C=C(C)[O-]","record_id":"MLDB-002656","method_description":"The lipophilicity of a compound is an important factor in determining ADMET (absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination, and toxicology) properties of drug substances. These properties impact drug distribution across the tissues and are measured by the octanol/water partition coefficient (log P values).42 The log P values of the complexes were determined using 2% DMSO–H2O with n-octanol. The partition coefficient P was determined by the ratio of a compound's concentration in the organic phase (octanol) to the concentration of the compound in the aqueous phase (water). The average log P values of the complexes [Co(III)-dione] and [Co(III)-dox] were 0.89 and 0.55, respectively, suggesting a relatively hydrophilic nature favouring solubility in the bloodstream."}]}